From 7f700682c8b5bfc8473b91e2b0919353494ca2cd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: homelab <homelab@emrys.dev>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 21:14:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 01/10] auth: trust Remote-User from an allowlisted proxy

A deployment behind Authelia's forward-auth has already proved who the
caller is; asking for the app's own password and Spotify cookies afterwards
is a second login, not a second factor. This adds the trusted-header mode
that removes it.

TRUSTED_HEADER_AUTH=1 makes Remote-User/Remote-Email the authenticated
identity. The account is created on first sight through the same allocator
the register form uses, so an existing standalone account with that email
keeps its history and stored cookies.

The security precondition is encoded, not assumed: TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS is
required, and the headers are believed only when the TCP peer is inside it.
The check uses the address ProxyFix saved before rewriting REMOTE_ADDR from
X-Forwarded-For, so a forged XFF cannot move a request into the trusted
range. A missing or malformed allowlist raises during construction rather
than starting a header-trust mode with no proxy constraint.

/login, /register and /reset-password answer 404 in this mode; /logout
returns to / and lets the proxy re-assert the session. /health and static
assets stay open for the kubelet and for rendering a refusal.

Unset - the default - leaves every upstream path untouched, which is what
keeps the fork a working standalone app.
